## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of multi-tenant isolation in cloud-native ML serving, per-tenant observability, and governance in AWS SageMaker.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant routing with PrivateLink for network isolation
- Per-tenant Feature Store namespaces for data isolation
- Canary deployments with automated rollback on drift or latency degradation
- Per-tenant monitoring, autoscaling, and governance via Model Registry

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Route inference request to tenant-specific endpoint
function routeToTenant(req, tenantId) {
  const endpoint = tenantEndpoints.get(tenantId);
  if (!endpoint) throw new Error('Unknown tenant');

  const result = await invokeEndpoint(endpoint, req.payload);

  // Check latency and trigger rollback if needed
  if (result.latencyP99 > latencyThreshold) {
    triggerAutoRollback(tenantId);
    alertOnCallers(tenantId, 'Latency spike detected');
  }

  return result;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates multi-tenant isolation, model versioning, and controlled rollout in real-time inference.

## Key Concepts
- SageMaker endpoint variants and traffic shifting
- SageMaker Model Registry and Pipelines for versioning
- Tenant-level routing and isolation, least-privilege IAM, PrivateLink
- Drift detection with SageMaker Clarify and per-tenant monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for per-tenant routing in the inference container
function route(event){
  const tenant = event.tenant_id;
  const variant = tenantVariantMap[tenant] || 'prod';
  // forward to the corresponding variant
  return invokeVariant(variant, event.payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback safety across tenants during a failed canary?
- How would you model per-tenant cost and scale limits in DynamoDB and CloudWatch?

